CAT = "auwahRisk"; SEG = "RSK"
Q = [
('What is the first step of the risk management process for falls?',
 'Identify the hazards', ['Control the main risks', 'Review the controls', 'Assess the risks'],
 'The process is identify hazards, assess risks if needed, control risks and review controls. You cannot control a fall hazard you have not found.'),
('In WHS terms, what is a hazard?',
 'Something that could cause harm', ['The chance of being hurt by it', 'A control that reduces the harm', 'An injury that has been reported'],
 'A hazard is a situation or thing that could harm a person, such as an open edge. Risk is the likelihood of harm from that hazard and how severe it could be.'),
('What does assessing the risk of a fall hazard involve?',
 'Judging likelihood and severity', ['Listing every tool on the site', 'Choosing the cheapest control', 'Counting only past injuries on site'],
 'A risk assessment considers what could happen if someone is exposed to the hazard, how serious the harm could be and how likely it is. It helps decide what to do and how urgently.'),
('Which roof surface should be treated as fragile?',
 'A fibreglass skylight', ['A new reinforced concrete slab', 'A steel roof truss', 'A plywood floor deck'],
 "Fibreglass sheeting and skylights, cement sheeting and rusty metal roofs can give way under a person's weight. Treat them as fragile unless they are protected or proven otherwise."),
('When can the formal risk assessment step be skipped?',
 'For a known risk with known controls', ['When the job is under one hour', 'When the work is below 4 metres', 'When the client accepts the risk in writing'],
 'If you already know the risk and how to control it effectively, you can go straight to implementing the controls. Short or low jobs still need their fall risks controlled.'),
('During a site walk, which of these is a fall hazard to record?',
 'An open stairwell void', ['A locked site office', 'A stack of bricks on the ground', 'A parked ute'],
 'Open edges, holes, voids, shafts and penetrations are classic fall hazards. Look for them in every work area and on the way in and out.'),
('Why can strong wind increase the fall risk on a roof?',
 'It can push workers off balance', ['It dries the roof surface faster', 'It cools the workers down', "It lowers the roof's pitch"],
 'Wind can push a worker off balance, especially when carrying roof sheets that act like sails. Weather, including rain and extreme heat or cold, is part of every fall risk assessment.'),
('Work is happening overhead. What best protects people below from falling objects?',
 'An exclusion zone below', ['Hi-vis vests for those below', 'Working faster overhead', 'A sign at the site office'],
 'A no go or exclusion zone with barriers and signs keeps people out of the drop zone. Toe-boards, mesh and tool lanyards reduce what can fall in the first place.'),
('Which is a main hazard of using a boom lift beside a street?',
 'Contact with powerlines', ['Poor phone reception', 'Glare from shop windows', 'Noise from passing traffic'],
 'The main EWP hazards are contact with electric lines, overturning, falls from the platform and crushing. Approach distances to powerlines vary by state.'),
('Can a fall from under 2 metres cause a serious injury?',
 'Yes, even low falls can kill', ['No, below 2 metres is safe', 'Only if you land on concrete', 'Only for workers over 50'],
 'Falls from ladders and low platforms cause many serious and fatal injuries. The 2 metre figure triggers a SWMS for construction work; it does not mean lower work is safe.'),
('Before setting up a mobile scaffold or EWP, what must you check about the ground?',
 'It is firm, level and stable', ['It has been swept and hosed', 'It is marked with painted lines', 'It is away from shade'],
 'Soft, uneven or sloping ground can make a scaffold or EWP tip. Check for pits, covers and penetrations that could give way under a wheel or base plate.'),
('Which records help a PCBU find fall hazards at a workplace?',
 'Past incident and near-miss reports', ['Payroll and annual leave records', "The site's material delivery dockets", 'Old supplier price lists'],
 'Records of past injuries, near misses and worker complaints show where falls have happened or nearly happened, so controls can target them.'),
('Why does the experience of the worker matter when assessing a fall risk?',
 'New workers may not know the dangers', ['Experienced workers can never fall', 'Training cannot reduce the risk', "Only a worker's age affects fall risk"],
 'Young, new or inexperienced workers may be unfamiliar with the work and its hazards, so they may need more training and supervision. Experience does not make anyone immune.'),
('Which of these is a fall hazard into, rather than from, a structure?',
 'An open trench', ['A mezzanine edge', 'A scaffold platform', 'A roof edge'],
 'Holes, shafts, pits and trenches present a risk of falling into them. Barriers or covers are needed just as for open edges.'),
('Why are wet glazed tiles a fall hazard at height?',
 'They are slippery', ['They are heavy to lift', 'They reflect the sun', 'They crack in the cold'],
 'Slippery surfaces such as glazed tiles, wet or oily surfaces make it hard to keep balance, especially on a slope. They also lower the safe working angle of a roof.'),
('You doubt a mezzanine floor can hold the materials you plan to stack on it. What should happen?',
 'Have an engineer check it', ['Spread the load and start work', 'Stack it near the edge instead', 'Ask a co-worker to test it'],
 'A surface must safely carry the expected loads of people, materials and equipment. When in doubt, a structural engineer should confirm the safe load before use.'),
('A new fall control brings a crushing hazard with it. What must happen?',
 'Control that hazard too', ['Accept it as part of the job', 'Swap back to no control', 'Report it after the job'],
 'Controls must not create new risks that are left unmanaged. If a control introduces a hazard, such as an EWP crushing risk, that hazard must also be controlled.'),
('A PCBU uses one generic fall risk assessment for several similar sites. When must it be reviewed?',
 "When a site's risks differ", ['Only every five years', 'Only after an injury occurs', 'Never, it covers all sites'],
 'A generic assessment can save time for similar work, but it must be reviewed if a site has different hazards or a person could be exposed to a different risk.'),
('Which weather condition should be part of a fall risk assessment?',
 'Rain making surfaces slippery', ['Humidity inside the site office', 'The forecast for next week', 'The phase of the moon'],
 'Rain, wind and extreme heat or cold can make surfaces slippery or conditions unstable. Check the weather before and during work at height.'),
('Why should footwear be considered when assessing a roof job?',
 'Poor grip makes slips likely', ['It sets the scaffold duty rating', 'It decides the licence class', 'It changes the roof pitch'],
 'Slip-resistant footwear suited to the surface and conditions helps workers keep their footing. Unsuitable soles make slips and falls more likely.'),
('Hot weather is forecast for a roofing job. Which planning step reduces the fall risk?',
 'Schedule height work for cooler hours', ['Remove the guardrails to let air through', 'Skip the pre-start meeting', 'Work alone to finish faster'],
 'Fatigue, heat and dehydration increase the chance of a fall. Sequencing work so height work avoids extreme heat or cold is a recognised control.'),
('Which feature of an incomplete building is a typical leading edge hazard?',
 'Unguarded formwork edges', ['A finished stair balustrade', 'A fenced site boundary', 'A completed ground slab'],
 'Leading edges such as formwork, incomplete stairwells and floor edges under construction often have no permanent protection yet. Plan edge protection before work reaches them.'),
('Before starting work at height, what should you inspect on site?',
 'Structures and site layout', ["The client's parking area", "The previous trade's invoices", 'The site office furniture'],
 "Inspecting the worksite's layout, the physical condition of structures and what equipment is needed lets you spot fall hazards and choose the right controls before work starts."),
('Which document sets out the steps, hazards and controls for a high risk construction task?',
 'The SWMS', ['The site plan', 'The timesheet', 'The delivery docket'],
 'The safe work method statement describes the high risk work, its hazards and risks, and how the controls will be implemented, monitored and reviewed. Read it before you start.'),
('A scaffold is to be erected within the no go zone of an overhead powerline. What is needed first?',
 "The network operator's approval", ["A verbal OK from the site's client", 'A warning sign fixed to the scaffold', 'A spotter for the first hour only'],
 'Work inside a powerline no go zone needs the permit or written approval set by the network operator. Distances and permit rules vary by state.'),
('Poor lighting on a stairway to a plant platform raises which risk?',
 'Missing a step and falling', ['The platform overheating', 'Damage to the light fittings', 'Electrical shock from the rail'],
 'Adequate natural or artificial lighting on access ways lets people see edges, steps and obstructions. Lighting is one of the factors in assessing fall risk.'),
('Why is a crowded, cluttered work platform a fall hazard?',
 'Trips can push people off edges', ['It slows the delivery of tools', "It raises the scaffold's height", 'It blocks mobile phone signal'],
 'Debris and materials on a platform cause trips and can knock edge protection or fall onto people below. Keep platforms clear along their length.'),
('Which place on a structure is noted as a hazard because hand grip may be lost?',
 'A wet steel ladder rung', ['A dry, textured stair handrail', 'A gripped tool belt', 'A padded EWP control'],
 'Places where hand grip may be lost, such as wet, oily or cold metal, are fall hazards. They may need a different access method or extra fall protection.'),
]
